      "M-INFO-106626": {
        "id": "M-INFO-106626",
        "title": "Engineering Self-Adaptive Systems",
        "credits": 3,
        "term": "WS",
        "termString": "Jedes Wintersemester",
        "duration": "1 Semester",
        "language": "English",
        "grading": "Zehntelnoten",
        "coordinators": [
          "Prof. Dr. Raffaela Mirandola"
        ],
        "organisation": "KIT-Fakultät für Informatik",
        "partOf": [],
        "categories": [
          "Electives"
        ],
        "applicableSpecializations": [],
        "isLab": false,
        "isThesis": false,
        "examType": "Examination",
        "prerequisites": "See partial achievements (Teilleistung)",
        "modeledPrerequisites": "",
        "exclusions": [],
        "requires": [],
        "competenceGoal": "- Understand the motivation for self-adaptation\n- Get familiar with the basic principles and conceptual model of self-adaptation\n- Understand how to engineer self-adaptive software systems from a software engineering perspective\n- Understand the decision-making process using formal analysis at runtime for quality assurance\n- Understand the notion of uncertainty in self-adaptive systems and how to tame it with formal verification at runtime\n- Understand the level of adoption of self-adaptive systems in industry.",
        "content": "Self-adaptation is an important field of research and engineering that aims to address the challenging problem of how to\nengineer software systems that have to deal with uncertainties that can only be resolved at run time.\nThe course presents the basic principles of self-adaptation and introduces a conceptual feedback loop model of a self-\nadaptive system. It introduces quality models which can be used to estimate quality properties at runtime by a self-\nadaptive system to provide guarantees for the quality goals. The role played by the different types of uncertainties is\nthen explored analyzing different possible approaches.",
        "workload": "90 hours workload.",
        "recommendations": "",
        "literature": ""
      },
      "M-INFO-107030": {
        "id": "M-INFO-107030",
        "title": "EU Data Protection Law",
        "credits": 3,
        "term": "WS",
        "termString": "Jedes Wintersemester",
        "duration": "1 Semester",
        "language": "English",
        "grading": "Zehntelnoten",
        "coordinators": [
          "Gustavo Gil Gasiola"
        ],
        "organisation": "KIT-Fakultät für Informatik",
        "partOf": [],
        "categories": [
          "Electives"
        ],
        "applicableSpecializations": [],
        "isLab": false,
        "isThesis": false,
        "examType": "Examination",
        "prerequisites": "See partial achievements (Teilleistung)",
        "modeledPrerequisites": "",
        "exclusions": [],
        "requires": [],
        "competenceGoal": "Students are able to comprehend the EU data protection regulation, including the General Data Protection Regulation\nand related EU data regulations.\nThey know the foundations of data protection rules, including fundamental concepts (e.g., “personal data”, “processing”,\n“data subject”). They are also familiar with the principles of personal data processing (lawfulness, limited purpose,\ntransparency, accountability) as well as the rights of the data subject.\nThey can identify the main obligations of the controller and the processor.\nStudents understand the conditions for the transfer of personal data to third countries.\nThey can identify the other regulations that govern data in the European Union.\nStudents are able to read and understand legal text related to data regulation.\nThey can understand and solve simple data protection cases.",
        "content": "The General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) of the European Union is a milestone in protecting individuals from the\nunlawful use of their data. In a data-driven society, economy, and government, this protection has become essential to\nguarantee fundamental rights. In addition to its direct impact on the legal systems of all Member States, the GDPR has a\nmajor influence on third countries that have adopted similar regulations (e.g. Switzerland, Argentina, Brazil, South Africa,\nand many others). In this way, the EU Data Protection Regulation has established itself as the “gold standard” of data\nprotection, providing guidance to address the challenges posed by new technologies and new ways of creating, using\nand sharing personal data. Understanding the structure of data protection in the EU is therefore essential to grasp its\nimpact on individual rights, public administration, business models, and even technological development.\nThis lecture aims to provide a structured overview of the EU Data Protection Regulation, and to offer tools to understand\nthe regulatory structure of the EU Data Regulation. The lecture will cover the following topics:\n- Introduction to EU law\n- Development of the EU data protection regulation\n- Legal structure of data protection in the EU\n- Role of national and sectoral laws\n- Data protection as fundamental right\n- Principles of data protection\n- Lawfulness of personal data processing\n- Anonymization and pseudonymization of personal data\n- Special categories of personal data\n- Rights of the data subject\n- Transfer of personal data to third countries\n- Responsibility of the controller and the processor\n- Security of personal data and personal data breach\n- Open Data Directive\n- Data Governance Act\n- Data Act",
        "workload": "90 hours workload.",
        "recommendations": "",
        "literature": ""
      },
