      "M-INFO-103306": {
        "id": "M-INFO-103306",
        "title": "Seminar: Proofs from THE BOOK",
        "credits": 3,
        "term": "WS+SS",
        "termString": "Unregelmäßig",
        "duration": "1 Semester",
        "language": "English",
        "grading": "Zehntelnoten",
        "coordinators": [
          "Prof. Dr. Peter Sanders"
        ],
        "organisation": "KIT-Fakultät für Informatik",
        "partOf": [],
        "categories": [
          "Electives"
        ],
        "applicableSpecializations": [],
        "isLab": false,
        "isThesis": false,
        "examType": "Examination",
        "prerequisites": "See partial achievements (Teilleistung)",
        "modeledPrerequisites": "",
        "exclusions": [],
        "requires": [],
        "competenceGoal": "The students learn to follow and understand complex mathematical proofs on their own. They learn to represent these\nproofs in an appealing manner and present the proofs to the other participants using a blackboard.\nStudents are familiar with the DFG Code of Conduct \"Guidelines for Safeguarding Good Scientific Practice\" and\nsuccessfully apply these guidelines in the preparation of their scientific work.",
        "content": "According to the Hungarian mathematician Paul Erdős, God is keeping a book – the BOOK – under wraps that contains\nthe most elegant mathematical proofs. Erdős’ loftiest goal was to find such proofs from the BOOK.\nAfter Erdős’ death in 1996, Martin Aigner and Günter Ziegler published the book “Proofs from THE BOOK” in 1998. The\nbook has also been published in German with the title “Das BUCH der Beweise”. In Aigner and Ziegler’s collection, there\nare some 40 of the most elegant proofs which are handled as candidates for BOOK-proofs.\nIn this seminar, the participants will present and discuss proofs from “Proofs from THE BOOK” and other well known and\nwell studied proofs in the area of mathematics and informatics.",
        "workload": "Seminar with 2 SWS, 3 LP\n3 LP correspond to about 90h of work, split into\nabout 20h attendance\nabout 60h preparation for seminar\nabout 10h follow-up",
        "recommendations": "",
        "literature": ""
      },
