      "M-PHYS-106842": {
        "id": "M-PHYS-106842",
        "title": "Advanced Topics in Quantum Field Theory",
        "credits": 4,
        "term": "WS+SS",
        "termString": "Irregular",
        "duration": "1 term",
        "language": "English",
        "grading": "graded",
        "coordinators": [
          "Prof. Dr. Ulrich Nierste",
          "Dr. Robert Ziegler"
        ],
        "organisation": "KIT Department of Physics",
        "partOf": [],
        "categories": [
          "Electives"
        ],
        "applicableSpecializations": [],
        "isLab": false,
        "isThesis": false,
        "examType": "Oral Exam. In the MSc Physics, this module is examined together with further modules attended as part of the major in\nphysics. The total duration of the oral exam is approx. 60 minutes.",
        "prerequisites": "None",
        "modeledPrerequisites": "",
        "exclusions": [],
        "requires": [],
        "competenceGoal": "Students learn or deepen their knowledge of important field theoretical concepts such as the treatment of symmetries in\npath integral formalism, effective theories using the example of chiral perturbation theory and quantum gravity, and chiral\nanomalies. In particular, after this lecture students will be familiar with the description of various physical phenomena by\neffective theories and their renormalization.",
        "content": "This module teaches various advanced concepts of quantum field theory as a supplement to the TTP lectures. As an\nintroduction, the path integral formalism is briefly reviewed and Dyson-Schwinger equations and Ward identities are\ndiscussed and used to prove gauge independence in QED non-perturbatively. This is followed by a discussion of effective\nfield theories, first conceptually and then using relevant examples such as chiral perturbation theory, Fermi theory and\nquantum gravity. Finally, chiral anomalies in the path integral formalism are treated and their significance for the standard\nmodel is discussed.",
        "workload": "120 hours workload.",
        "recommendations": "",
        "literature": ""
      },
      "M-PHYS-106196": {
        "id": "M-PHYS-106196",
        "title": "Array Techniques in Seismology (Graded)",
        "credits": 4,
        "term": "WS+SS",
        "termString": "Irregular",
        "duration": "1 term",
        "language": "English",
        "grading": "graded",
        "coordinators": [
          "apl. Prof. Dr. Joachim Ritter"
        ],
        "organisation": "KIT Department of Physics",
        "partOf": [],
        "categories": [
          "Electives"
        ],
        "applicableSpecializations": [],
        "isLab": false,
        "isThesis": false,
        "examType": "Grading is based on written reports on exercises. A detailed rating scheme is distributed during the first lecture together\nwith information on the required length of the reports and rating criteria.",
        "prerequisites": "None",
        "modeledPrerequisites": "",
        "exclusions": [],
        "requires": [],
        "competenceGoal": "The students understand basic principles of array techniques. This includes the increase in signal-to-noise ratio due to\nstacking or beamforming and the estimation of simple shear-wave velocity profiles. They know how to determine the\nslowness or ray parameter of an incoming wavefield as well as its backazimuth. These parameters are used to estimate the\nlocation of a seismic source. Furthermore, they know how to divide different phase arrivals using a vespagram or an f-k\nanalysis.\nThe students are able to work self-organized on a specific issue of array seismology, e.g., the location of a nuclear test or\nthe local shear-wave velocity structure underneath a local array. They are able to read and understand technical and\nscientific literature on array seismology. They can outline and analyze seismological cases in which array techniques can\nsolve specific problems such as seismic phase identification or source location estimation.",
        "content": "•\n•\n•\n•\n•\n•\nFundamentals of seismic waves\nMeasurable parameters of seismic waves using arrays\nDetermination of source locations\nDetermination of underground properties\nGlobal seismic arrays and their role for monitoring nuclear tests and earthquakes\nTraining on array software and application to seismological data sets",
        "workload": "Total workload: 120h which consist of 15h lecture at GPI, 15h reading of research papers and lecture material, 15h\npreparation and wrap-up of lecture, 15h guided exercise in the computing room at GPI to learn about array software (basic\nLinux and Python knowledge required), 30h self-organized training with array software and application to data sets, and 30h\npreparation of reports on exercises.",
        "recommendations": "Participants need to know the basics of seismology.",
        "literature": ""
      },
      "M-PHYS-102075": {
        "id": "M-PHYS-102075",
        "title": "Astroparticle Physics I",
        "credits": 8,
        "term": "WS",
        "termString": "Each winter term",
        "duration": "1 term",
        "language": "English",
        "grading": "graded",
        "coordinators": [
          "Prof. Dr. Guido Drexlin",
          "Prof. Dr. Kathrin Valerius"
        ],
        "organisation": "KIT Department of Physics",
        "partOf": [],
        "categories": [
          "Electives"
        ],
        "applicableSpecializations": [],
        "isLab": false,
        "isThesis": false,
        "examType": "Oral Exam. In the MSc Physics, this module is examined together with further modules attended as part of the major in\nphysics. The total duration of the oral exam is approx. 60 minutes.",
        "prerequisites": "none",
        "modeledPrerequisites": "The following conditions have to be fulfilled:\n1.\nThe module M-PHYS-102076 - Astroparticle Physics I (Minor) must not have been started.",
        "exclusions": [],
        "requires": [],
        "competenceGoal": "Students will be introduced to the basic concepts of astroparticle physics. The lecture teaches both the theoretical concepts\nand the experimental methods of this new dynamic field of work at the interface of elementary particle physics, cosmology\nand astrophysics. Students will learn to understand the concepts through concrete case studies from current research and\nwill be enabled to apply the learned methods independently.\nMethodological skills acquisition:\n•\n•\n•\n•\nUnderstanding of the fundamentals of experimental astroparticle physics.\nRecognition of methodological cross-connections to elementary particle physics, astrophysics, and cosmology.\nAcquisition of the ability to present a current research topic independently as well as in a team setting\nAcquisition of the ability to implement the concepts and experimental methods in the master thesis",
        "content": "The topics covered include a general introduction to the field with its fundamental issues, theoretical concepts and\nexperimental methods. Corresponding to the very different energy scales (meV - 1020 eV) of astroparticle physics, the\nlecture is divided into a discussion of the processes in the thermal (low energies) and non-thermal (high energies) universe.\nA special focus of the lecture is a comprehensive presentation of modern experimental techniques, e.g. in the search for\nvery rare processes. Based on this, in the second part of the lecture a comprehensive introduction to the \"dark universe\"\nand the search for dark matter is given.\nThe lecture is the basis of further lectures on this topic (Astroparticle Physics II).",
        "workload": "exercises(180 hours)\n240 hours consisting of attendance time (60 hours), wrap-up of the lecture incl. exam preparation and preparation of the",
        "recommendations": "Basic knowledge from the lecture \"Nuclei and Particles\".",
        "literature": ""
      },
