      "M-PHYS-105683": {
        "id": "M-PHYS-105683",
        "title": "Astroparticle Physics II - Gamma Rays and Neutrinos",
        "credits": 6,
        "term": "SS",
        "termString": "Each summer term",
        "duration": "1 term",
        "language": "English",
        "grading": "graded",
        "coordinators": [
          "Prof. Dr. Ralph Engel"
        ],
        "organisation": "KIT Department of Physics",
        "partOf": [],
        "categories": [
          "Electives"
        ],
        "applicableSpecializations": [],
        "isLab": false,
        "isThesis": false,
        "examType": "Oral Exam. In the MSc Physics, this module is examined together with further modules attended as part of the major in\nphysics. The total duration of the oral exam is approx. 60 minutes.",
        "prerequisites": "none, the lecture is designed complementary to the module Astroparticle Physics I and can be heard independently of it",
        "modeledPrerequisites": "The following conditions have to be fulfilled:\n1.\nThe module M-PHYS-105684 - Astroparticle Physics II - Gamma Rays and Neutrinos (Minor) must not have been\n2.\n3.\nstarted.\nThe module M-PHYS-105685 - Astroparticle Physics II - Gamma Rays and Neutrinos, with ext. Exercises (Minor) must\nnot have been started.\nThe module M-PHYS-105686 - Astroparticle Physics II - Gamma Rays and Neutrinos, with ext. Exercises must not have\nbeen started.",
        "exclusions": [],
        "requires": [],
        "competenceGoal": "After successful participation in this module, the student has an in-depth technical and survey knowledge in the field of\nhigh-energy astroparticle physics. He/she understands the most important formation processes of gamma rays and\nneutrinos, is able to interpret observed energy spectra of astrophysical objects and has basic knowledge of the astrophysics\nof galactic and extragalactic sources of high-energy particles.",
        "content": "The fundamentals of astroparticle physics involving high-energy particles will be discussed, with emphasis on the\napplication of gamma and neutrino astronomy to the study of astrophysical objects. Starting with the acceleration of\ncharged particles, the first third of the lecture series introduces the main formation processes of gamma radiation,\ndiscusses the propagation of high-energy gamma radiation, and presents methods for detecting gamma radiation on Earth\nand in space. The second third of the lecture series discusses astrophysical objects and their image in gamma rays:\nsupernova explosions and remnants, neutron stars and pulsars, black holes and Active Galactic Nuclei, and gamma-ray\nbursts. The course is rounded out by an introduction to the fundamentals and current issues in astronomy involving high-\nenergy neutrinos.\nTogether with the course \"Astroparticle Physics II: Cosmic Rays\", which is offered in the WS, a complete picture of high-\nenergy particles with their underlying production and transport processes in our universe is obtained. The subject spectra\nof both lectures are complementary in nature and can be heard independently, but complement each other appropriately.\nThe lecture ATP II \" Gamma Rays and Neutrinos\" is complementary to further in-depth lectures (Astroparticle Physics II \"\nCosmic Rays\" or \"Particles and Stars\").",
        "workload": "exercises (135 hours).\n180 hours consisting of attendance time (45 hours), wrap-up of the lecture incl. exam preparation and preparation of the\nPhysics Master (Master of Science)\n4 MODULES Module: Astroparticle Physics II - Gamma Rays and Neutrinos [M-PHYS-105683]",
        "recommendations": "Basic knowledge of the physics of particles and nuclei and of experimental methods in this area is assumed.",
        "literature": ""
      },
