      "M-PHYS-106530": {
        "id": "M-PHYS-106530",
        "title": "Block Practical Course: ETP Data Science",
        "credits": 2,
        "term": "WS",
        "termString": "Each winter term",
        "duration": "1 term",
        "language": "English",
        "grading": "pass/fail",
        "coordinators": [
          "Prof. Dr. Torben Ferber",
          "Prof. Dr. Jan Kieseler",
          "Prof. Dr. Markus Klute"
        ],
        "organisation": "KIT Department of Physics",
        "partOf": [],
        "categories": [
          "Electives"
        ],
        "applicableSpecializations": [],
        "isLab": false,
        "isThesis": false,
        "examType": "The regular attendance of the entire block course is required. The successful completion will be evaluated by a short oral\ntest on the preparatory work and a final presentation in the week after the course.",
        "prerequisites": "None (preparatory material and exercises will be sent around in advance of the course)",
        "modeledPrerequisites": "",
        "exclusions": [],
        "requires": [],
        "competenceGoal": "The students are familiar with the basic concepts of calorimetry, the simulation of particle showers, and the use of machine\nlearning for the determination of the incident particle energy. This includes the interaction of high energetic particles with\nmatter, the evolution of electromagnetic and hadronic showers through the material, and the detection of signals for\ndetermining the original particle energy. The students know different neural network architectures in addition to classical\nmethods for energy reconstruction based on these signals.\nThe theoretical course content, tutorials and practical training are combined and designed to enable students to develop an\nintuitive understanding of the advantages and disadvantages of different calorimeter types for high energy physics\nexperiments. Furthermore, they can simulate the response of those calorimeters with state-of-the art simulation software,\nexplore different geometries, and are able to understand, choose, and train suitable neural network architectures for energy\nreconstruction hands-on.",
        "content": "•\n•\n•\n•\nIntroduction to high-energy physics calorimetry\nHands-on simulation of calorimeter designs with the Geant4 simulation software\nHands-on implementation of neural network building blocks\nApplication of advanced neural networks to particle energy reconstruction in calorimeters\nAdditional Information\nsecond major in physics.\nThis module cannot be combined with an advanced seminar or any other non-graded module in the major in physics or",
        "workload": "60 hours consisting of preparatory work (15 hours) in advance to the course start, an attendance time (30 hours) during the\none-week block course with lectures, tutorials and a practical training, and a preparation of a final presentation (15 hours)\nafter the block course.",
        "recommendations": "Basic knowledge of python and neural networks is helpful",
        "literature": ""
      },
      "M-PHYS-104862": {
        "id": "M-PHYS-104862",
        "title": "Computational Condensed Matter Physics",
        "credits": 12,
        "term": "WS+SS",
        "termString": "Irregular",
        "duration": "1 term",
        "language": "English",
        "grading": "graded",
        "coordinators": [
          "Prof. Dr. Wolfgang Wenzel"
        ],
        "organisation": "KIT Department of Physics",
        "partOf": [],
        "categories": [
          "Electives"
        ],
        "applicableSpecializations": [],
        "isLab": false,
        "isThesis": false,
        "examType": "Oral Exam. In the MSc Physics, this module is examined together with further modules attended as part of the major in\nphysics. The total duration of the oral exam is approx. 60 minutes.",
        "prerequisites": "none",
        "modeledPrerequisites": "The following conditions have to be fulfilled:\n1.\nThe module M-PHYS-104863 - Computational Condensed Matter Physics (Minor) must not have been started.",
        "exclusions": [],
        "requires": [],
        "competenceGoal": "In recent decades, simulation has established itself as a third pillar of research alongside analytical theory and experiment.\nIt often bridges the gap from principled insights to applications to specific systems. Students develop and gain knowledge\nof materials-specific simulation for condensed matter systems, from ordered solids to soft matter. Students become\nfamiliar with available simulation techniques and apply them to specific problems in condensed matter. They acquire key\nskills in the use of open-source software to solve simulation problems in condensed matter, in autonomy, in synthesizing\nthe results of different methods for a holistic description in the simulation of material properties.",
        "content": "•\n•\n•\n•\n•\n•\n•\nQuantum mechanics of many-particle systems\nMethods of quantum chemistry (LCAO, Hartree Fock, density functional theory, electron correlations)\nApplications to molecules and solids\nSimulation methods for classical many-particle systems (Monte Carlo, molecular dynamics)\nApplications to structure formation in polymers, glasses, and solids.\nIntroduction to multiscale simulations (QM/MM, multilevel methods) and artificial intelligence techniques.\nModeling of electronic transport",
        "workload": "360 hours consisting of attendance time (60 hours lecture, 30 hours exercises), follow-up of the lecture incl. exam\npreparation and working on the exercises (270 hours)",
        "recommendations": "",
        "literature": ""
      },
