      "M-PHYS-107500": {
        "id": "M-PHYS-107500",
        "title": "Dark Matter – Theoretical Aspects",
        "credits": 6,
        "term": "WS+SS",
        "termString": "Irregular",
        "duration": "1 term",
        "language": "English",
        "grading": "graded",
        "coordinators": [
          "Prof. Dr. Thomas Schwetz-Mangold"
        ],
        "organisation": "KIT Department of Physics",
        "partOf": [],
        "categories": [
          "Electives"
        ],
        "applicableSpecializations": [],
        "isLab": false,
        "isThesis": false,
        "examType": "Oral Exam. In the MSc Physics, this module is examined together with further modules attended as part of the (second)\nmajor in physics. The total duration of the oral exam is approx. 60 minutes.",
        "prerequisites": "none",
        "modeledPrerequisites": "The following conditions have to be fulfilled:\n1.\nThe module M-PHYS-107501 - Dark matter – Theoretical Aspects (Minor) must not have been started.",
        "exclusions": [],
        "requires": [],
        "competenceGoal": "Students are familiar with the interdisciplinary problem of dark matter. The material learned includes various aspects of\nparticle physics, cosmology, gravitation, and astrophysics, as well as the phenomenology of astroparticle physics\nexperiments. Students understand the cosmological observations that point to the existence of dark matter. They can\ndescribe the various possible particle candidates for dark matter and understand the essential aspects of the underlying\nparticle physics models.",
        "content": "The lecture provides an introduction to the problem of dark matter (DM). The astrophysical and cosmological observations\nthat point to the existence of DM are discussed, such as structure formation in the universe. The phenomenology for the\ndifferent methods to search for DM (direct search, indirect search, search at accelerator experiments) is discussed. Particle\nphysics models of the various candidates for DM are introduced, including WIMPs (Weakly Interacting Massive Particles),\nwarm DM (sterile neutrinos) or axions. In the different cases, the problem of how to produce the correct density of particles\nin the early universe is addressed.",
        "workload": "(135 h)\n180 h consisting of attendance time (45 h), follow-up of the lecture incl. exam preparation and completion of the exercises",
        "recommendations": "",
        "literature": ""
      },
