      "M-PHYS-102175": {
        "id": "M-PHYS-102175",
        "title": "Introduction to Cosmology",
        "credits": 6,
        "term": "WS",
        "termString": "Each winter term",
        "duration": "1 term",
        "language": "English",
        "grading": "graded",
        "coordinators": [
          "Prof. Dr. Guido Drexlin",
          "Prof. Dr. Kathrin Valerius"
        ],
        "organisation": "KIT Department of Physics",
        "partOf": [],
        "categories": [
          "Electives"
        ],
        "applicableSpecializations": [],
        "isLab": false,
        "isThesis": false,
        "examType": "Oral Exam. In the MSc Physics, this module is examined together with further modules attended as part of the major in\nphysics. The total duration of the oral exam is approx. 60 minutes.\nModeled Prerequisites\nThe following conditions have to be fulfilled:\n1.\nThe module M-PHYS-102176 - Introduction to Cosmology (Minor) must not have been started.",
        "prerequisites": "The following conditions have to be fulfilled:\n1.\nThe module M-PHYS-102176 - Introduction to Cosmology (Minor) must not have been started.",
        "modeledPrerequisites": "The following conditions have to be fulfilled:\n1.\nThe module M-PHYS-102176 - Introduction to Cosmology (Minor) must not have been started.",
        "exclusions": [],
        "requires": [],
        "competenceGoal": "Students will be introduced to the basic concepts of cosmology. The lecture will provide both the theoretical concepts and\nan overview of modern experimental methods and observational techniques. The students will be enabled to understand\nthe concepts by means of concrete case studies from modern cosmology and will be enabled to apply the learned methods\nin the context of later independent research.\nMethodological Competency Acquisition:\n•\n•\n•\nUnderstanding of the fundamentals of cosmology\nRecognition of methodological cross-connections to elementary particle physics and astroparticle physics.\nAcquisition of the ability to work independently on current research topics as preparation for the master thesis.",
        "content": "The lecture offers an introduction to modern cosmology, which has taken an enormous upswing in recent years due to the\nuse of state-of-the-art technologies (Planck satellite, galaxy surveys such as 2dF and SDSS) and accompanying\ncomputationally intensive simulations (Millennium). The large number of observations has led to the establishment of a so-\ncalled concordance model of cosmology, in which the contributions of dark energy and dark matter dominate the evolution\nof large-scale structures in the universe.\nStarting from a description of the early universe with the supporting pillars of the Big Bang theory (Hubble expansion,\nnucleosynthesis, cosmic background radiation) and the phase transitions and symmetry breaking that occur in the process,\nthe formation and evolution of large-scale structures in the universe up to today's \"dark universe\" is discussed (comparison\nof \"top-down\" with \"bottom-up\" models). Special attention is given to a detailed presentation of the most modern\nexperimental techniques and methods of analysis, which have found their way into wide areas of physics.\nThe lecture thus provides a coherent picture of modern cosmology and discusses fundamental issues also in neighboring\ndisciplines such as particle physics and astrophysics and can therefore be complemented with other lectures in the field of\nExperimental Astroparticle Physics and Experimental Particle Physics.",
        "workload": "exercises (135 hours).\n180 hours consisting of attendance time (45 hours), wrap-up of the lecture incl. exam preparation and preparation of the",
        "recommendations": "Basic knowledge from lecture \"Nuclei and Particles",
        "literature": ""
      },
