      "M-PHYS-102368": {
        "id": "M-PHYS-102368",
        "title": "Inversion and Tomography",
        "credits": 8,
        "term": "SS",
        "termString": "Each summer term",
        "duration": "1 term",
        "language": "English",
        "grading": "graded",
        "coordinators": [
          "Prof. Dr. Thomas Bohlen",
          "apl. Prof. Dr. Joachim Ritter"
        ],
        "organisation": "KIT Department of Physics",
        "partOf": [],
        "categories": [
          "Electives"
        ],
        "applicableSpecializations": [],
        "isLab": false,
        "isThesis": false,
        "examType": "To pass the module, an oral exam must be passed (approx. 20 min). As prerequisites the examinations of other type must be\npassed, based on successful participation of the exercises. Students write reports on their exercise work. These reports are\nrated. The necessary number of points is explained at the beginning of the individual exercises.",
        "prerequisites": "none",
        "modeledPrerequisites": "The following conditions have to be fulfilled:\n1.\nThe module M-PHYS-102658 - Inversion and Tomography (Minor) must not have been started.",
        "exclusions": [],
        "requires": [],
        "competenceGoal": "The students understand how to invert data to achieve a model of physical parameters. The students realize that seismic\nwaves can be treated in different waves: full waveform, finite-frequency approximations (banana-doughnut theory) and\nrays. From this they understand how seismic images can be constructed and interpreted. Students are able to evaluate\ninversion models based on error bonds, resolution matrices and reconstruction tests. They know the complete chain of\ntomography: data pre-processing, parameterization, inversion, model assessment and interpretation. The students are used\nto read scientific papers on inversion and tomography and to discuss questions on these papers. Finally the students are\nable to understand basic inverse problems and read more advanced texts. Practically, the students understand how to code\nsimple problems with Matlab or possibly Python. The students know how to analyze inverse problems using singular value\ndecomposition and other methods.",
        "content": "•\n•\n•\n•\n•\n•\nFundamentals of tomography\nApplication of seismic tomography\nRegional to global seismic tomography\nAnalysis of tomography problems\nFundamentals in seismic inversion\nApplication of linear and non-linear inversion",
        "workload": "240 hours composed of attendance time (60 h), wrap-up of the lectures and solving the exercises (180 h)",
        "recommendations": "Knowledge on fundamentals of seismology and understanding of mathematics, especially matrix calculus. Fundamental\nskills in Linux, Matlab and computing in general.",
        "literature": ""
      },
