      "M-PHYS-107689": {
        "id": "M-PHYS-107689",
        "title": "Path Integrals in Quantum Mechanics and Quantum Field Theory",
        "credits": 2,
        "term": "WS+SS",
        "termString": "Irregular",
        "duration": "1 term",
        "language": "English",
        "grading": "graded",
        "coordinators": [
          "Prof. Dr. Ulrich Nierste",
          "Dr. Robert Ziegler"
        ],
        "organisation": "KIT Department of Physics",
        "partOf": [],
        "categories": [
          "Electives"
        ],
        "applicableSpecializations": [],
        "isLab": false,
        "isThesis": false,
        "examType": "For use as a major/second major subject:\nOral examination. As part of the major/second major subject of the MSc Physics, the module is examined together with\nother modules taken. The duration of the oral examination is approx. 60 minutes.",
        "prerequisites": "Modern Theoretical Physics I (Fundamentals of Quantum Mechanics)",
        "modeledPrerequisites": "",
        "exclusions": [],
        "requires": [],
        "competenceGoal": "The aim of the lecture is the in-depth treatment of central concepts in quantum mechanics, such as Schrödinger equation,\nclassical limit case, and harmonic oscillator with the help of the path integral formalism. Furthermore, the lecture is\nintended to serve as preparation for (or for supplementary understanding of) quantum field theory by introducing\nperturbation theory and Green's functions with the quantum mechanical path integral to motivate their application and\nmeaning in field theory. After this lecture, students will be familiar with the basic theoretical and practical aspects of path\nintegrals in quantum mechanics and quantum field theory.",
        "content": "This module conveys the motivation, theoretical foundations and application of the path integral formalism in quantum\nmechanics and quantum field theory. After an introduction, the quantum mechanical path integral is explained using the\nexample of the free particle and the harmonic oscillator, the equivalence to the Schrödinger formalism is shown and the\nlimiting case to classical mechanics is demonstrated. Subsequently, perturbation theory and Green's functions are\nintroduced with the quantum mechanical path integral, and their significance and application in scalar field theory are\ndiscussed. Finally, instantons and the fermionic path integral are discussed.",
        "workload": "60 hours consisting of attendance time (15 hours), wrap-up of the lecture incl. exam preparation (45 hours).",
        "recommendations": "Theoretical Particle Physics I",
        "literature": ""
      },
      "M-ETIT-100513": {
        "id": "M-ETIT-100513",
        "title": "Photovoltaics",
        "credits": 6,
        "term": "SS",
        "termString": "Each summer term",
        "duration": "1 term",
        "language": "German",
        "grading": "graded",
        "coordinators": [
          "Prof. Dr.-Ing. Michael Powalla"
        ],
        "organisation": "KIT Department of Electrical Engineering and Information Technology",
        "partOf": [],
        "categories": [
          "Electives"
        ],
        "applicableSpecializations": [],
        "isLab": false,
        "isThesis": false,
        "examType": "examination.\nSuccess is assessed in the form of a written examination (2 hours). The module grade is the grade of this written",
        "prerequisites": "Module \"M-ETIT-100524 - Solar Energy\" must not have started.\nPhysics Master (Master of Science)\n4 MODULES Module: Photovoltaics [M-ETIT-100513]",
        "modeledPrerequisites": "",
        "exclusions": [],
        "requires": [],
        "competenceGoal": "For the lecture Photovoltaics with 3 SWS lecture and 1 SWS exercise, the following competence requirements are specified\nby the following objectives:\nA.Specialist knowledge:\nAfter attending the course, students will be able to:\n•\n•\n•\n•\n•\nunderstand energy conversion in semiconductors. They will be able to analyze the physical description of light and\nthe interaction of light with solids. Students will gain knowledge about the energy conversion of different forms of\nenergy and the transport of electrical energy in semiconductors and metals. They will be able to describe and\nmathematically represent the functioning of p/n diodes.\ndiscuss the associated current technological and production-related issues. In particular, students examine the\ntechnical implementation of semiconductor requirements in technical processes. They gain knowledge of the entire\nvalue chain (physical principles, material science aspects, production engineering applications and systemic\nintegration)\nunderstand photovoltaic energy systems in the interaction of all components. The comparison of the systemic\nintegration of off-grid and grid-integrated solar-based energy generation systems helps to explain the components\nand their design. The system quality, efficiency, costs etc. can be explained with the help of key figures.\nStudents quantify the loss mechanisms in the solar cell in the solar converter and the solar systems, in particular to\noptimize economic and ecological key figures, and learn about operating experience and long-term stability issues.\nunderstand how different solar cell technologies and solar thermal energy conversion work and how to classify them\nin an overall energy system\nB. Research and problem-solving skills:\nThe students (after participating in the course)\n•\n•\n•\nare able to think in an interdisciplinary way. Basic skills from physics, electrical engineering and information\ntechnology, production engineering and economics are brought together and complement each other to form an\noverall picture.\nare familiar with methods for analyzing systems composed of discrete components,\nare familiar with state-of-the-art methods of describing energy conversion systems using solar primary energy,\nC. Assessment and planning skills:\nStudents (after participating in the course)\n•\n•\n•\nare able to assess and categorize different solar cell concepts and different solutions for solar power generation,\nrecognize the limits and challenges of providing electrical energy from locally and temporally fluctuating sources\nand can thus initiate new developments,\nscrutinize new concepts in the highly dynamic field of solar electrical energy generation in connection with climate\nprotection and security of supply\nD. Personal and social skills:\nThe students (after participating in the course)\n•\n•\n•\nare familiar with the derivation and origin of the most important physical relationships and recognize the synergy of\ndifferent scientific disciplines,\ncan calculate tasks independently and communicate the results orally and in writing,\nrecognize the relevance of technical solutions for climate protection",
        "content": "This module is designed to help students understand energy conversion in semiconductors. Photovoltaic energy systems\nare dealt with in the interaction of all components and loss mechanisms in the solar cell and in the photovoltaic system are\nquantified. The functionality of solar thermal energy generation is taught. In addition, the associated current technological\nand production-related issues are discussed.",
        "workload": "Calculation basis: 15 lecture weeks\n1.\n2.\n3.\n4.\n5.\n6.\nLecture attendance time: 23 * 1.5 h = 34.5 h\nPreparation and follow-up time for lecture: 23 * 2 h = 46 h\nExercise 7 x 1.5 h = 10.5 h.\nPreparation and follow-up time for exercise: 7 x 4 h = 28 h\nExcursion 10 h\nExam preparation and attendance (2 h): 51 h\nTotal = 180 h",
        "recommendations": "",
        "literature": ""
      },
