      "M-PHYS-106193": {
        "id": "M-PHYS-106193",
        "title": "Quantum Detectors and Sensors",
        "credits": 8,
        "term": "WS",
        "termString": "Each winter term",
        "duration": "1 term",
        "language": "English",
        "grading": "graded",
        "coordinators": [
          "Prof. Dr. Sebastian Kempf"
        ],
        "organisation": "KIT Department of Electrical Engineering and Information Technology KIT Department of Physics",
        "partOf": [],
        "categories": [
          "Electives"
        ],
        "applicableSpecializations": [],
        "isLab": false,
        "isThesis": false,
        "examType": "Oral Exam. In the MSc Physics, this module is examined together with further modules attended as part of the major in\nphysics. The total duration of the oral exam is approx. 60 minutes.",
        "prerequisites": "none",
        "modeledPrerequisites": "",
        "exclusions": [],
        "requires": [],
        "competenceGoal": "Students know the basics and fundamentals of quantum detectors and sensors and understand how quantum technology\ncan be used to design and realize devices those performance reaches far beyond the limits of any classical sensor or\ndetector. They know the basic components of quantum sensors and detectors, in particular in the field of superconducting\nquantum technology, and are able to analyze the operation of such detectors and sensors on the basis of circuit diagrams.\nStudents are able to develop quantum sensors and detectors for given applications and know how to consider special\nrequirements in a concrete component.",
        "content": "This module provides a comprehensive overview of the basics and physical principles of quantum detectors and sensors\nand discusses in detail how quantum technology can be used to design and realize detectors and sensors with performance\nthat reaches far beyond the limits of any classical sensor or detector. The discussion includes particularly an introduction to\nthe basic components of quantum sensors and detectors, especially in the field of superconducting quantum technology,\nand their fabrication. Using simplified circuit diagrams, the functionality and operation of quantum detectors and sensors\nsuch as superconducting quantum interference devices, low-temperature detectors, noise thermometers or\nsuperconducting radiation detectors is analyzed. Furthermore, methods and simple models are developed allowing to\nrealize quantum sensors and detectors that are matched to given applications. Within this context, typical applications of\nquantum detectors and sensors are also discussed.\nThe tutorial is closely related to the lecture and deals with special aspects concerning the development of quantum\ndetectors and sensors. In particular, the development and system integration of quantum detectors and sensors for\napplications in precision metrology, particle detection or applied sciences is discussed by means of exercises.\nAdditional Information\nGerman.\nThe lecture and exercise will be offered in English. However, questions and discussions can of course also be held in",
        "workload": "exercises (180 hours)\n240 hours consisting of attendance time (60 hours), follow-up of the lecture incl. exam preparation and working on the",
        "recommendations": "",
        "literature": ""
      },
