      "M-PHYS-104186": {
        "id": "M-PHYS-104186",
        "title": "Seismic Data Processing with Final Report (Graded)",
        "credits": 6,
        "term": "WS+SS",
        "termString": "Irregular",
        "duration": "1 term",
        "language": "English",
        "grading": "graded",
        "coordinators": [
          "Prof. Dr. Thomas Bohlen",
          "Dr. Thomas Hertweck"
        ],
        "organisation": "KIT Department of Physics",
        "partOf": [],
        "categories": [
          "Electives"
        ],
        "applicableSpecializations": [],
        "isLab": false,
        "isThesis": false,
        "examType": "Students have to participate the lecture/exercise on a regular basis and give a final presentation about their processing\nresults (2 ECTS points). Students who would like to get the full 6 ECTS points also need to prepare and hand in a seismic\ndata processing report. The report will determine the final grade (if applicable).",
        "prerequisites": "None",
        "modeledPrerequisites": "",
        "exclusions": [],
        "requires": [],
        "competenceGoal": "The students have hands-on experience applying typical seismic processing and imaging techniques to reflection seismic\nfield data. In this way, they understand the reflection seismic method and have practical skills in data analysis and problem\nsolving which are beneficial in their professional life later on, not only in exploration. Students can set up a basic processing\nand imaging flow, understand the individual processing steps and their purpose, and describe the influence of important\nparameters on processing results. They are able to identify data shortcomings and imaging challenges and develop basic\nsolutions, analyze the success of individual processing/imaging steps, and critically assess the overall quality of their work.\nFinally, students are able to present their processing results in oral and written form.",
        "content": "•\n•\n•\n•\n•\n•\n•\n•\n•\n•\nField data loading, quality control, trace edits and geometry setup\nSpectral analysis, filter application, geometrical spreading correction\nDeconvolution, zero-phasing\nDenoising using various approaches\nMultiple identification and removal (SRME, Radon)\nCMP sort, velocity analysis, NMO correction, mute and stack\nTime migration (prestack and poststack)\nPost-migration processing\nBasic interpretation (in cooperation with KIT-AGW)\nOptional: depth velocity model building and depth migration",
        "workload": "180 h hours composed of contact time (45 h), wrap-up of the lectures and solving the exercises (135 h)",
        "recommendations": "No explicit requirements. However, basic knowledge of the reflection seismic method and general computer skills are\nessential. This course does not require any programming skills.",
        "literature": ""
      },
      "M-PHYS-105227": {
        "id": "M-PHYS-105227",
        "title": "Seismic Modeling",
        "credits": 4,
        "term": "SS",
        "termString": "Each summer term",
        "duration": "1 term",
        "language": "English",
        "grading": "graded",
        "coordinators": [
          "Prof. Dr. Thomas Bohlen"
        ],
        "organisation": "KIT Department of Physics",
        "partOf": [],
        "categories": [
          "Electives"
        ],
        "applicableSpecializations": [],
        "isLab": false,
        "isThesis": false,
        "examType": "To pass the module, the oral exam (approx. 20 minutes) must be passed. As prerequisites the examinations of other type\nmust be passed, based on successful participation of the exercises. Each exercise deals with a specific topic (e.g., 1D finite-\ndifference implementation) and is based on hands-on work, usually involving the use of computers.",
        "prerequisites": "none",
        "modeledPrerequisites": "The following conditions have to be fulfilled:\n1.\nThe module M-PHYS-105228 - Seismic Modeling (Minor) must not have been started.",
        "exclusions": [],
        "requires": [],
        "competenceGoal": "The students know the fundamental concepts of seismic wavefield simulations, including the mathematical descriptions and\ntheir basic numeric implementations. They understand the complexity of wave propagation and the advantages and\ndisadvantages of the individual methods. They are able to apply the methods using synthetic Earth models to calculate\namplitudes and traveltimes of propagating elastic and/or acoustic waves.",
        "content": "•\n•\n•\n•\n•\n•\n•\n•\nFactors influencing traveltimes and amplitudes of seismic wavefields\nAnalytical solutions\nFast traveltime calculation using the eikonal equation\nRaytracing\nReflectivity method for acoustic 1D media\nTime-domain finite-difference solutions of the acoustic/elastic wave equations\nFourier methods\nIntroduction to the finite-element method",
        "workload": "120 hours workload.",
        "recommendations": "Knowledge of differential and vector calculus is essential. Familiarity with Matlab (alternatively Python or Mathematica) is\nbeneficial for certain exercises.",
        "literature": ""
      },
