      "M-PHYS-106833": {
        "id": "M-PHYS-106833",
        "title": "Software Engineering in Condensed Matter Physics",
        "credits": 6,
        "term": "WS+SS",
        "termString": "Irregular",
        "duration": "1 term",
        "language": "English",
        "grading": "graded",
        "coordinators": [
          "Prof. Dr. Wolfgang Wenzel"
        ],
        "organisation": "KIT Department of Physics",
        "partOf": [],
        "categories": [
          "Electives"
        ],
        "applicableSpecializations": [],
        "isLab": false,
        "isThesis": false,
        "examType": "Oral Exam. In the MSc Physics, this module is examined together with further modules attended as part of the major in\nphysics. The total duration of the oral exam is approx. 60 minutes.",
        "prerequisites": "none",
        "modeledPrerequisites": "The following conditions have to be fulfilled:\n1.\nThe module M-PHYS-106834 - Software Engineering in Condensed Matter Physics (Minor) must not have been\nstarted.",
        "exclusions": [],
        "requires": [],
        "competenceGoal": "In recent decades, simulation has established itself as a third pillar of research alongside analytical theory and experiment,\nbut the complexity of the underlying software is steadily increasing. In order to bridge this gap students in this course\nacquire skills in advanced software engineering in python with application to simulation problems in condensed matter\nsystems, from ordered solids to soft matter. They are familiar with object-oriented programming, rapid prototyping, test-\ndriven development, the use and development of python libraries and basics in machine learning (scikit/pytorch). In\naddition workflow technologies and data-driven model development are known.",
        "content": "•\n•\n•\n•\n•\n•\n•\nObject Oriented Programming techniques in Python (Software Patterns)\nRapid prototyping, test-driven development\nIntroduction to machine learning (scikit: classification, interpolation)\nNeural Networks (Dense, Deep, Recursive, ...)\nData-driven model development (Materials Project)\nWorkflow Environments (pymatgen, simstack)\nApplications to problem in condensed matter physics",
        "workload": "180 hours consisting of attendance time (30 hours lecture, 15 hours exercises), follow-up of the lecture incl. exam\npreparation and working on the exercises (135 hours)",
        "recommendations": "",
        "literature": ""
      },
