      "M-PHYS-104857": {
        "id": "M-PHYS-104857",
        "title": "Solid State Quantum Technologies",
        "credits": 8,
        "term": "WS+SS",
        "termString": "Irregular",
        "duration": "1 term",
        "language": "English",
        "grading": "graded",
        "coordinators": [
          "Prof. Dr. Wolfgang Wernsdorfer"
        ],
        "organisation": "KIT Department of Physics",
        "partOf": [],
        "categories": [
          "Electives"
        ],
        "applicableSpecializations": [],
        "isLab": false,
        "isThesis": false,
        "examType": "Oral Exam. In the MSc Physics, this module is examined together with further modules attended as part of the major in\nphysics. The total duration of the oral exam is approx. 60 minutes.",
        "prerequisites": "none",
        "modeledPrerequisites": "The following conditions have to be fulfilled:\n1.\nThe module M-PHYS-104858 - Solid State Quantum Technologies (Minor) must not have been started.",
        "exclusions": [],
        "requires": [],
        "competenceGoal": "The development and comprehensive use of Quantum Technology is one of the most ambitious technological goals of\ntoday's science,with expected dramatic impact on the whole society and economy. The field of quantum information\nprocessing using solid state quantum bits (qubits) has witnessed an exponential growth during the last years. The current\nperformances suggest that within a horizon of a few years, solid state quantum machines could outperform even the best\nclassical machines for a few types of particularly hard tasks. During this class, the students will acquire a basic\nunderstanding of the principles of quantum information processing and the functioning of computers based on qubits, with\nan emphasis on experimental implementations using superconducting circuits and cavities and spin based solid state\nqubits. The supporting problems will cover in detail a broad set of calculations, from derivations of basic results, to solving\npractical problems one could encounter in a research laboratory.",
        "content": "After a general introduction to the concepts of quantum information processing, we will present an overview of different\nexperimental implementations. We will then focus on spin qubits and superconducting circuit qubits. We will discuss\nsources of loss and dephasing, and we will mention several strategies to increase the coherence of qubits. During the last\nfew lectures, we will focus on advanced topics such as circuit quantum electrodynamics (cQED) and quantum optics in the\nmicrowave domain.",
        "workload": "exercises (180 hours).\n240 hours consisting of attendance time (60 hours), wrap-up of the lecture incl. exam preparation and working on the",
        "recommendations": "Basic knowledge of quantum mechanics",
        "literature": ""
      },
