      "M-PHYS-102984": {
        "id": "M-PHYS-102984",
        "title": "The ABC of DFT",
        "credits": 6,
        "term": "WS+SS",
        "termString": "Irregular",
        "duration": "1 term",
        "language": "English",
        "grading": "graded",
        "coordinators": [
          "Prof. Dr. Carsten Rockstuhl",
          "Prof. Dr. Wolfgang Wenzel"
        ],
        "organisation": "KIT Department of Physics",
        "partOf": [],
        "categories": [
          "Electives"
        ],
        "applicableSpecializations": [],
        "isLab": false,
        "isThesis": false,
        "examType": "Oral Exam. In the MSc Physics, this module is examined together with further modules attended as part of the major in\nphysics. The total duration of the oral exam is approx. 60 minutes.",
        "prerequisites": "none",
        "modeledPrerequisites": "",
        "exclusions": [],
        "requires": [],
        "competenceGoal": "Understanding of basic numerical methods in density functional theory and the ability to apply them to solve physical\nproblems in solid state physics such as the description of charge transport or magnetism. Emphasis is placed on acquiring\nthe skills for independent simulation execution, subsequent data analysis, physical interpretation and, if possible, linkage\nwith experimental investigations.",
        "content": "With ever advancing computational power, it becomes possible to determine the electronic structure of increasingly\ncomplex systems relevant to solid state physics and materials science. Here we introduce Density Functional Theory (DFT) by\nexplaining the basic underlying concepts, present examples of its application and its shortcomings and outline the most\npromising improvement paths. DFT will be applied to charge transport and magnetism related problems. As DFT makes it\npossible to treat fairly large systems (up to a few thousand of electrons) it enables direct comparison to experiment for\nmany important applications. Both periodic, crystalline systems and localized single molecule in vacuum will be addressed\nwith a special focus on systems with reduced dimensionality, such as surfaces and interfaces. Where applicable,\ncomparisons to experiment and possible deployments will be presented. Some of the topics that will be addressed are:\n•\n•\n•\n•\n•\nBasic concepts underpinning the DFT\nCalculations of band structure and density of states (DOS) of (hybrid) graphene materials.\nTreatment of magnetism within DFT, with examples of both bulk and molecular magnetism.\nCharge transport, with examples of both ballistic and disordered hopping transport.\nBeyond ground state DFT: Time Dependent DFT, GW, ...",
        "workload": "h)\n180 h consisting of attendance time (60 h), wrap-up of the lecture incl. exam preparation and working on the exercises (120",
        "recommendations": "Basic knowledge of solid state theory, quantum mechanics, and thermodynamics is assumed.",
        "literature": ""
      },
      "M-PHYS-106845": {
        "id": "M-PHYS-106845",
        "title": "Theoretical Cosmology, with Exercises",
        "credits": 12,
        "term": "SS",
        "termString": "Each summer term",
        "duration": "1 term",
        "language": "English",
        "grading": "graded",
        "coordinators": [
          "Prof. Dr. Felix Kahlhöfer",
          "Prof. Dr. Thomas Schwetz-Mangold"
        ],
        "organisation": "KIT Department of Physics",
        "partOf": [],
        "categories": [
          "Electives"
        ],
        "applicableSpecializations": [],
        "isLab": false,
        "isThesis": false,
        "examType": "Oral Exam. In the MSc Physics, this module is examined together with further modules attended as part of the major in\nphysics. The total duration of the oral exam is approx. 60 minutes.\nModeled Prerequisites\nThe following conditions have to be fulfilled:\n1.\n2.\n3.\n4.\nThe module M-PHYS-104855 - Introduction to Theoretical Cosmology must not have been started.\nThe module M-PHYS-104856 - Introduction to Theoretical Cosmology (Minor) must not have been started.\nThe module M-PHYS-106846 - Theoretical Cosmology, with Exercises (Minor) must not have been started.\nThe module M-PHYS-106847 - Theoretical Cosmology, without Exercises must not have been started.",
        "prerequisites": "The following conditions have to be fulfilled:\n1.\n2.\n3.\n4.\nThe module M-PHYS-104855 - Introduction to Theoretical Cosmology must not have been started.\nThe module M-PHYS-104856 - Introduction to Theoretical Cosmology (Minor) must not have been started.\nThe module M-PHYS-106846 - Theoretical Cosmology, with Exercises (Minor) must not have been started.\nThe module M-PHYS-106847 - Theoretical Cosmology, without Exercises must not have been started.",
        "modeledPrerequisites": "The following conditions have to be fulfilled:\n1.\n2.\n3.\n4.\nThe module M-PHYS-104855 - Introduction to Theoretical Cosmology must not have been started.\nThe module M-PHYS-104856 - Introduction to Theoretical Cosmology (Minor) must not have been started.\nThe module M-PHYS-106846 - Theoretical Cosmology, with Exercises (Minor) must not have been started.\nThe module M-PHYS-106847 - Theoretical Cosmology, without Exercises must not have been started.",
        "exclusions": [],
        "requires": [],
        "competenceGoal": "Students are familiar with different aspects of the Big Bang theory. They understand the basic physical concepts and can\napply relevant methods of theoretical physics to cosmology. They know about selected advanced topics in cosmology.\nParticipants of the course can apply the concepts and techniques they have learned to solve selected problems in\nCosmology.",
        "content": "The lecture gives an introduction to the standard model of cosmology, the so-called LCDM model. The fundamental physics\nprinciples of the model are discussed. Starting from fundamental theories such as general relativity, particle physics,\nthermodynamics and statistical physics, we derive the properties and predictions of the LCDM model. We consider the\nexpansion of the Universe, dark matter, dark energy, thermodynamics in the early Universe, Big Bang nucleosynthesis,\ncosmic structure formation, cosmic microwave background radiation. Selected advanced topics include the theory of cosmic\ninflation, cosmological phase transitions, baryogenesis, neutrino cosmology, cosmological gravitational waves, dark matter\ncandidates.",
        "workload": "preparation (270 hours).\n360 hours consisting of attendance time (90 hours), wrap-up of the lecture incl. preparation of the exercises and exam",
        "recommendations": "Basic knowledge of General Relativity is recommended, but all required concepts will be introduced. Basic knowledge of\nparticle physics and quantum field theory is helpful.",
        "literature": ""
      },
