      "M-PHYS-102295": {
        "id": "M-PHYS-102295",
        "title": "Theoretical Nanooptics",
        "credits": 6,
        "term": "WS+SS",
        "termString": "Irregular",
        "duration": "1 term",
        "language": "English",
        "grading": "graded",
        "coordinators": [
          "Prof. Dr. Markus Garst",
          "Prof. Dr. Carsten Rockstuhl"
        ],
        "organisation": "KIT Department of Physics",
        "partOf": [],
        "categories": [
          "Electives"
        ],
        "applicableSpecializations": [],
        "isLab": false,
        "isThesis": false,
        "examType": "Oral Exam. In the MSc Physics, this module is examined together with further modules attended as part of the major in\nphysics. The total duration of the oral exam is approx. 60 minutes.",
        "prerequisites": "none",
        "modeledPrerequisites": "The following conditions have to be fulfilled:\n1.\nThe module M-PHYS-103177 - Theoretical Nanooptics (Minor) must not have been started.",
        "exclusions": [],
        "requires": [],
        "competenceGoal": "The properties of light at the nanoscale can be controlled by various means. The aim of this lecture is to familiarize the\nstudents with the different possibilities that rely on nanostructured dielectric or metallic materials and to outline on solid\nmathematical grounds the analytical description of observable effects. The lecture is meant as a complementary source of\neducation to experimental lecture. It shall provide the students with the necessary skills to work themselves in the field of\ntheoretical nanooptics.",
        "content": "•\n•\n•\n•\n•\n•\n•\n•\nDispersion relation to describe light in extended systems such as free space, interfaces, planar waveguides and\nwaveguides with complicated geometrical cross sections.\nDescription of the interaction of light with isolated objects such as spheres, cylinders, ellipsoids and prolates and\noblates.\nProperties of plasmonic nanoparticles and the ability to tune their properties\nNotion of optical antennas and the discussion of their basic characteristics\nDescription of the dynamics of wave propagation by perturbed eigenstates, i.e. coupled mode theory. Application to\noptical waveguide arrays.\nDiscussion of metamaterials (unit cells, homogenization, light propagation, applications)\nTransformation optics\nAnalytical modeling and phenomenological tools to describe nanooptical systems",
        "workload": "180 hours composed of active time (45), wrap-up of the lecture incl. preparation of the examination and the excercises (135)",
        "recommendations": "Solid mathematical background, good knowledge of classical electromagnetism and theoretical optics.\nPhysics Master (Master of Science)\n4 MODULES Module: Theoretical Nanooptics [M-PHYS-102295]",
        "literature": ""
      },
