      "M-PHYS-105942": {
        "id": "M-PHYS-105942",
        "title": "Theory and Applications of Quantum Machines",
        "credits": 8,
        "term": "WS+SS",
        "termString": "Irregular",
        "duration": "1 term",
        "language": "English",
        "grading": "graded",
        "coordinators": [
          "Prof. Dr. Anja Metelmann"
        ],
        "organisation": "KIT Department of Physics",
        "partOf": [],
        "categories": [
          "Electives"
        ],
        "applicableSpecializations": [],
        "isLab": false,
        "isThesis": false,
        "examType": "Oral Exam. In the MSc Physics, this module is examined together with further modules attended as part of the major in\nphysics. The total duration of the oral exam is approx. 60 minutes.",
        "prerequisites": "None",
        "modeledPrerequisites": "",
        "exclusions": [],
        "requires": [],
        "competenceGoal": "The students know the possible applications of quantum technologies and understand the operation of key core\narchitectures such as superconducting circuits. Students understand the detrimental effect of dissipation on the operation\nand performance of quantum technologies, and they learn possible protocols to avoid dissipation. Students learn about\nvarious readout elements and protocols and understand the fundamental quantum mechanical limitations of\nmeasurements. Students understand the relevant basic concepts in the field of superconducting circuits, such as cavity,\nqubit, dispersive readout, fidelity, etc., as well as the basic concepts of optomechanical architectures, such as sidebands,\ndynamic feedback, fundamental limits on measurement accuracy, etc. Students are able to analyze, structure, and formally\ndescribe simple problems in the area of open quantum systems. Simple problems here include a two-level system or a\nmechanical mode coupled to the light field of a cavity. Students are able to apply the methodology of the Heisenberg-\nLangevin equations as well as that of the master equation. Students are able to perform the calculation of noise spectra of\nthese example systems. Students will learn the modern methodologies of modeling open quantum systems, e.g. the\nformalism of quantum trajectories, feedback protocols and quasi-distributions.",
        "content": "This module aims to provide students with the theoretical and practical aspects of modern quantum technologies. Different\ntechnological architectures will be covered, e.g. superconducting circuits as a basis for future efficient computers,\noptomechanical systems as a basis for increasing the sensitivity of force sensors, or spin-based quantum communication\nsystems. The module will cover the basic concepts of theoretical modeling of open quantum systems, with a focus on\nquantum mechanical measurement and readout. The influence of dissipation as well as the fundamental limits of\nmeasurement accuracy will be addressed. The module will provide an overview of future applications of quantum\ntechnologies, and at the same time highlight the challenges that these technologies face.",
        "workload": "exercises (180 h)\n240 hours consisting of attendance time (60 h), wrap-up of the lecture incl. exam preparation and preparation of the",
        "recommendations": "",
        "literature": ""
      },
