      "M-PHYS-107540": {
        "id": "M-PHYS-107540",
        "title": "Theory and Phenomenology of the QCD Axion",
        "credits": 2,
        "term": "WS+SS",
        "termString": "Irregular",
        "duration": "1 term",
        "language": "English",
        "grading": "graded",
        "coordinators": [
          "Prof. Dr. Ulrich Nierste",
          "Dr. Robert Ziegler"
        ],
        "organisation": "KIT Department of Physics",
        "partOf": [],
        "categories": [
          "Electives"
        ],
        "applicableSpecializations": [],
        "isLab": false,
        "isThesis": false,
        "examType": "For use as a major/second major subject:\nOral examination. As part of the major/second major subject of the MSc Physics, the module is examined together with\nother modules taken. The duration of the oral examination is approx. 60 minutes.",
        "prerequisites": "Theoretical Particle Physics I",
        "modeledPrerequisites": "The following conditions have to be fulfilled:\n1.\nThe module M-PHYS-107543 - Theory of Condensed Matter: Quantum Field Theory and Quantum Criticality (Minor)\nmust not have been started.",
        "exclusions": [],
        "requires": [],
        "competenceGoal": "The students deepen important field-theoretical concepts such as anomalous symmetries and effective theories using the\nexample of the QCD axion, and learn the essential phenomenological properties of light new particles. After this lecture, the\nstudents are familiar with the importance of light axions as a solution to the strong CP problem and dark matter, as well as\nwith the basics of astrophysical constraints and the most important experimental searches.",
        "content": "This module provides an introduction to the theory and phenomenology of light new particles beyond the Standard Model\nusing the QCD axion as an example. After a brief outline of the Standard Model, the strong CP problem is explained and the\nQCD axion is introduced as probably the most elegant proposed solution. The discussion of the theoretical foundations is\nfollowed by a compact overview of the most important phenomenological properties of the QCD axion. After describing\ngeneral axion couplings in the context of an effective field theory, the structure of explicit models is briefly outlined before\nthe significance of the QCD axion as dark matter is discussed in detail. The cosmological aspects are followed by a\ndiscussion of the astrophysical constraints before finally discussing dedicated experimental searches for axions with helio-\nand haloscopes such as CAST or ADMX.",
        "workload": "60 hours consisting of attendance time (15 hours), wrap-up of the lecture incl. exam preparation (45 hours).",
        "recommendations": "Theoretical Particle Physics II",
        "literature": ""
      },
