      "M-PHYS-102367": {
        "id": "M-PHYS-102367",
        "title": "Theory of Seismic Waves",
        "credits": 6,
        "term": "SS",
        "termString": "Each summer term",
        "duration": "1 term",
        "language": "English",
        "grading": "graded",
        "coordinators": [
          "Prof. Dr. Thomas Bohlen"
        ],
        "organisation": "KIT Department of Physics",
        "partOf": [],
        "categories": [
          "Electives"
        ],
        "applicableSpecializations": [],
        "isLab": false,
        "isThesis": false,
        "examType": "To pass the module, an oral exam must be passed (approx. 20 min). As prerequisites the examinations of other type must be\npassed, based on successful participation of the exercises. Each exercise deals with a specific topic (e.g., stress and strain\ntensors, Zoeppritz equations, or rays) and is based on solving a given theoretical problem by means of calculus. In some\ncases equations and solutions need to be visualized using Matlab (or equivalent tools).",
        "prerequisites": "none",
        "modeledPrerequisites": "The following conditions have to be fulfilled:\n1.\nThe module M-PHYS-102657 - Theory of Seismic Waves (Minor) must not have been started.",
        "exclusions": [],
        "requires": [],
        "competenceGoal": "The students know the fundamental laws and equations of linear elasticity and wave propagation. They understand wave\npropagation phenomena such as source effects, reflection and transmission or the effects of anisotropy, absorption,\ndispersion and scattering and can describe them in mathematical terms. They are able to apply the concepts and equations\nto theoretical problems and relate the theory to phenomena observed in field data.",
        "content": "•\n•\n•\n•\n•\n•\n•\n•\n•\n•\nTheory of elasticity, stress and strain, elastic tensor, fundamental laws and equations\nAnisotropic elastic wave equation and various simplifications\nMathematical description of sources, near-field and far-field terms\nBoundary conditions\nReflection and transmission of plane waves at plane interfaces, Zoeppritz equations\nSurface waves, dispersion relation, phase and group velocity\nIntroduction to ray theory, eikonal and transport equations and their solutions\nAbsorption and dispersion\nWave propagation in anisotropic media\nScattering",
        "workload": "180 hours composed of attendance time (45 h), wrap-up of the lectures and solving the exercises (135 h)",
        "recommendations": "Knowledge of differential and vector calculus is essential. Familiarity with Matlab (alternatively Python or Mathematica) is\nbeneficial for certain exercises.",
        "literature": ""
      },
