      "M-INFO-106504": {
        "id": "M-INFO-106504",
        "title": "Simulation and Optimization in Robotics and Biomechanics",
        "credits": 6,
        "term": "WS",
        "termString": "Jedes Wintersemester",
        "duration": "1 Semester",
        "language": "English",
        "grading": "Zehntelnoten",
        "coordinators": [
          "Prof. Dr. Katja Mombaur"
        ],
        "organisation": "KIT-Fakultät für Informatik",
        "partOf": [],
        "categories": [
          "Electives"
        ],
        "applicableSpecializations": [],
        "isLab": false,
        "isThesis": false,
        "examType": "Examination",
        "prerequisites": "See partial achievements (Teilleistung)",
        "modeledPrerequisites": "",
        "exclusions": [],
        "requires": [],
        "competenceGoal": "The students\n• can explain advanced principles of modeling, optimization and control of dynamic processes, in particular mechanical\nsystems and can apply them\n• can model, classify and analyze complex motions in robotics or biomechanics, and investigate specific properties such as\nstability.\n• can apply nonlinear optimization and optimal control methods and can compare and evaluate different mathematical\napproaches.\n• know how to use software tools based on C++ and Lua for modeling, simulation, optimization and visualization of\nhumanoid and robotic systems\nare capable of solving optimal control problems numerically and to evaluate the quality of the solution.",
        "content": "The goal of this course is to give a practical introduction into simulation and optimization of motions in robotics and\nbiomechanics. Simulation and optimization play an important role in generating and controlling motions in complex\nrobotics systems and in predicting and analyzing motions of humans. Theory and methods will be covered, but the focus is\non the use software tools for modeling, simulation, optimization and visualization of multibody systems. Topics covered\ninclude:\n• Dynamic process modeling\n• Transforming real world problems into mathematical models\n• Modeling of complex robotics and biomechanics systems (e.g. humanoids), based on previous modeling knowledge\n• Common template models for bipedal walking and running in robotics and biomechanics\n• Simulation of mechanical / robotics systems (Integrators and Initial value problems)\n• Boundary value problems\n• Nonlinear optimization problems\n• Optimal control problems\n• Direct and indirect methods for optimal control problems, focus on direct methods, especially direct multiple shooting\n• Stability of dynamical systems, stability in biomechanics and robotics\nAnmerkungen\nLimitation to 30 participants",
        "workload": "180 hours workload.",
        "recommendations": "",
        "literature": ""
      },
